{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9d9becdc18ebb2be", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t \n2\t\n3\t AudioScape-TTA: A Structured Soundscape Benchmark for Fine-Grained Text-to-Audio Evaluation \n4\t\n5\t Report GitHub Issue \n6\t × \n7\t\n8\t Title: \n9\t\n10\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n11\t\n12\t Description: \n13\t\n14\t Submit without GitHub \n15\t Submit in GitHub \n16\t\n17\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n18\t Learn more \n19\t &times; \n20\t\n21\t Back to arXiv \n22\t\n23\t Why HTML? \n24\t\n25\t Report Issue \n26\t\n27\t Back to Abstract \n28\t\n29\t Download PDF \n30\t\n31\t Abstract \n32\t Introduction \n33\t\n34\t Related Work \n35\t\n36\t Text-to-Audio Generation \n37\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. \n38\t\n39\t AudioScape-TTA \n40\t\n41\t Overview \n42\t Structured Soundscape Benchmark \n43\t Rubric-based Audio-grounded Evaluation \n44\t\n45\t Experiments \n46\t\n47\t Experimental Setup \n48\t Fine-grained Semantic Evaluation \n49\t Comparison with Conventional Evaluation Metrics \n50\t User Study \n51\t Human Alignment of Evaluation Metrics \n52\t Discussion \n53\t\n54\t Conclusion \n55\t References \n56\t\n57\t Additional Dataset Details \n58\t\n59\t Annotation Pipeline \n60\t\n61\t Complexity Score Definition \n62\t\n63\t Event Density. \n64\t Structural Complexity. \n65\t Complexity Score. \n66\t Complementarity Analysis. \n67\t\n68\t Data Statistics \n69\t\n70\t Semantic Rubrics Construction Details \n71\t\n72\t Rubrics Types. \n73\t Structured rubrics schema. \n74\t Target generation. \n75\t Question rewriting. \n76\t Leakage prevention and fallback templates. \n77\t Validation and filtering. \n78\t\n79\t Detailed Metric Definitions \n80\t\n81\t Semantic Satisfaction Rate. \n82\t Evaluator confidence. \n83\t Speech-content preprocessing. \n84\t Mixed-unit coverage. \n85\t Speech Content satisfaction rate. \n86\t Supplementary WER and CER diagnostics. \n87\t Modality, composition, and complexity subsets. \n88\t Complementary global metrics. \n89\t\n90\t Human Evaluation Protocol \n91\t\n92\t TTA Model Generation Protocol \n93\t\n94\t Evaluation Input and Runtime Setup \n95\t Per-Model Generation Protocol \n96\t Model Architecture and Design Observations \n97\t\n98\t Additional Experimental Results \n99\t\n100\t Modality-combination Analysis \n101\t Audio Quality Evaluation \n102\t\n103\t Additional Speech Content Evaluation \n104\t\n105\t Additional Speech-Content Diagnostics. \n106\t Speech-content coverage threshold sensitivity. \n107\t Correlation between SCCA and WER. \n108\t\n109\t Sanity Checks for Audio-Grounded Rubric-Based Evaluation \n110\t\n111\t Audio-conditioned controls. \n112\t Evaluator capability check. \n113\t\n114\t Evaluator Bias and Answer-Position Analysis \n115\t\n116\t Balanced reference-audio sensitivity analysis. \n117\t Answer-position sensitivity. \n118\t Scope of contrastive negatives. \n119\t\n120\t License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0\n121\t\n122\tarXiv:2608.04479v1 [cs.SD] 05 Aug 2026 \n123\t\n124\t AudioScape-TTA: A Structured Soundscape Benchmark for Fine-Grained Text-to-Audio Evaluation \n125\t\n126\tJinting Wang 1 ,\n127\tYuguang Yang 2 ,\n128\tShengyu Li 1 ,\n129\tYan Rong 1 ,\n130\t Shan Yang 2 ,\n131\tXiaoda Yang 3 ,\n132\tLi Liu 1 \n133\t\n134\t Abstract \n135\t Text-to-audio (TTA) generation has recently achieved remarkable progress in\n136\tsynthesizing realistic audio from natural language descriptions. However,\n137\tdetermining whether generated audio faithfully satisfies complex textual\n138\tinstructions remains challenging. Existing benchmarks mainly rely on global\n139\tsimilarity metrics, providing limited insight into fine-grained semantic\n140\tfailures. To address this limitation, we introduce AudioScape-TTA , a\n141\tstructured and complexity-aware benchmark for fine-grained TTA evaluation.\n142\tAudioScape-TTA represents realistic soundscapes through modality-aware\n143\tsemantic structures and characterizes generation complexity using event\n144\tdensity and structural complexity. Based on these annotations, we propose a\n145\trubric-based audio-grounded evaluation framework that verifies event\n146\trealization, acoustic attributes, and speech content through fine-grained\n147\tsemantic criteria. The benchmark contains 2,258 audio-text pairs with 25,707\n148\tbinary QA rubrics, enabling scalable and interpretable analysis of TTA\n149\tsystems. Experiments on 13 representative open-source TTA models reveal\n150\tpersistent limitations in fine-grained attribute control, speech-content\n151\tpreservation, and compositional soundscape generation. Human validation\n152\tfurther demonstrates that our rubric-based evaluation achieves stronger\n153\talignment with human semantic judgments than conventional global similarity\n154\tmetrics. \n155\t\n156\t Figure 1: Comprehensive evaluation of open-source TTA generation models on the AudioScape-TTA benchmark.\n157\t(a) Semantic ability evaluation across three audio modalities, including sound, music, and speech generation.\n158\tSpeech satisfaction rate includes both speaker-related attributes and content correctness.\n159\t(b) Complexity generalization performance under different generation complexity levels, including easy, medium, and hard subsets.\n160\tThe \n161\t\n162\t ★ \\bigstar \n163\t\n164\t ★ {\\color[rgb]{1,0.7578125,0.02734375}\\definecolor[named]{pgfstrokecolor}{rgb}{1,0.7578125,0.02734375}\\bigstar} \n165\t symbol denotes the best-performing model for each metric. \n166\t\n167\t Introduction \n168\t\n169\t Recent advances in text-to-audio (TTA) generation have enabled models to synthesize diverse audio components, including sound effects, music, and speech, from textual instructions  (Vyas et al. 2023 ; Yang et al. 2023 ) .\n170\tWith improved generation quality and controllability, TTA systems are increasingly expected to handle complex instructions that describe multiple interacting audio components rather than isolated sound events.\n171\tTherefore, the evaluation challenge is shifting from measuring general audio plausibility toward determining whether models can faithfully realize realistic structured soundscapes . \n172\t\n173\t However, existing TTA benchmarks provide limited support for evaluating structured soundscape generation. First, existing benchmarks do not consistently provide realistic and compositional instructions that explicitly represent heterogeneous audio components within a single scene. As summarized in Table  1 , prior studies investigate caption-level generation, conditional generation, temporal control, and event-level relations  (Kim et al. 2019 ; Drossos et al. 2020 ; Xie et al. 2025 ; He et al. 2025 ; Wang et al. 2026 , 2025a ) . However, their prompts are largely centered on coarse-grained sound events, offering limited support for jointly specifying sound effects, background music, and speech within a unified soundscape.\n174\tIn particular, speech is often treated as an audio event rather than a linguistic modality with target utterance constraints, limiting the evaluation of speech-content preservation in complex soundscapes. \n175\t\n176\t Second, existing benchmarks provide limited complexity-aware analysis of structured soundscape generation. Although recent studies consider multi-event prompts and event-level relations, evaluation is typically summarized as aggregate performance. Such evaluation does not characterize sample-level complexity, which may vary with acoustic content, active audio categories, and their clip-level composition. Consequently, existing benchmarks provide limited insight into how semantic instruction following varies as soundscape complexity increases. \n177\t\n178\t Beyond benchmark construction, reliable automatic evaluation remains challenging. Existing metrics primarily rely on global text–audio similarity, such as CLAP  (Wu et al. 2023 ) , or holistic judgments from powerful audio-language models. While these approaches provide useful overall assessments, they typically do not explicitly verify whether individual semantic requirements in structured soundscape instructions are satisfied. Consequently, generated audio may receive favorable global scores while still missing specific components. This motivates a fine-grained evaluation system that provides interpretable diagnoses of semantic failures. \n179\t\n180\t To address these limitations, we introduce AudioScape-TTA , a structured and complexity-aware benchmark for fine-grained TTA evaluation. AudioScape-TTA contains 2,258 instances and 25,707 semantic rubrics.\n181\tEach sample is annotated as a structured soundscape comprising scene context, sound effects, background music, and speech components, together with fine-grained semantic rubrics for semantic verification.\n182\tWe characterize sample complexity through two complementary dimensions: event density , which measures the amount of acoustic content in a clip, and structural complexity , which characterizes the diversity and clip-level composition of active audio categories.\n183\tThis design enables analysis of model robustness across soundscapes with different levels of acoustic density and compositional complexity. \n184\t\n185\t Building upon this benchmark, we propose an audio-grounded rubrics-based evaluation framework that decomposes each structured soundscape instruction into fine-grained semantic requirements covering event presence, acoustic attributes, and speech content. The framework evaluates these requirements through audio-language understanding and transcription-based verification, providing interpretable diagnostics beyond global text–audio similarity metrics. It further supports fine-grained analysis across audio modalities, soundscape compositions, and complexity levels. \n186\t\n187\t We evaluate 13 open-source TTA models spanning diverse generation paradigms. Results show that global similarity and perceptual-quality metrics provide complementary but incomplete indications of fine-grained semantic following ability. Complexity-aware analysis reveals robustness differences across soundscapes with varying complexity. At the model level, the proposed rubrics-based evaluation achieves stronger correlation with human semantic judgments than CLAP-based similarity, demonstrating the effectiveness of structured semantic assessment. \n188\t\n189\t Our contributions are summarized as follows: \n190\t\n191\t • \n192\t\n193\t We introduce AudioScape-TTA , a structured and complexity-aware benchmark that represents realistic audio scenes as structured soundscapes and enables fine-grained evaluation across different complexity. \n194\t\n195\t • \n196\t\n197\t We propose a rubric-based audio-grounded evaluation that decomposes semantic fidelity into event presence, acoustic attributes, and speech content, enabling scalable and interpretable evaluation. \n198\t\n199\t • \n200\t\n201\t We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of 13 open-source TTA models, revealing key limitations in fine-grained semantic instruction following. \n202\t\n203\t Benchmark \n204\t\n205\t Samples \n206\t\n207\t Structured \n208\t Schema \n209\t\n210\t Speech \n211\t Content \n212\t\n213\t Semantic \n214\t Diagnosis \n215\t\n216\t Complexity \n217\t Aware \n218\t\n219\t AudioCaps 1.0 (Kim et al. 2019 ) \n220\t\n221\t 975 \n222\t × \\times \n223\t × \\times \n224\t × \\times \n225\t × \\times \n226\t\n227\t AudioCaps 2.0 (Kim et al. 2019 ) \n228\t\n229\t 1,778 \n230\t × \\times \n231\t × \\times \n232\t × \\times \n233\t × \\times \n234\t\n235\t Clotho (Drossos et al. 2020 ) \n236\t\n237\t 1,045 \n238\t × \\times \n239\t × \\times \n240\t × \\times \n241\t × \\times \n242\t\n243\t AudioCondition (Guo et al. 2024 ) \n244\t\n245\t 1,110 \n246\t △ \\triangle \n247\t × \\times \n248\t △ \\triangle \n249\t × \\times \n250\t\n251\t AudioTime (Xie et al. 2025 ) \n252\t\n253\t 500 \n254\t △ \\triangle \n255\t × \\times \n256\t △ \\triangle \n257\t △ \\triangle \n258\t\n259\t RiTTA (He et al. 2025 ) \n260\t\n261\t 720 \n262\t △ \\triangle \n263\t × \\times \n264\t △ \\triangle \n265\t △ \\triangle \n266\t\n267\t AudioAtlas (Wang et al. 2025a ) \n268\t\n269\t 1,165 \n270\t △ \\triangle \n271\t × \\times \n272\t △ \\triangle \n273\t × \\times \n274\t\n275\t VinTAGe-Bench (Kushwaha and Tian 2025 ) \n276\t\n277\t 636 \n278\t △ \\triangle \n279\t × \\times \n280\t △ \\triangle \n281\t × \\times \n282\t\n283\t TTA-Bench (Wang et al. 2026 ) \n284\t\n285\t 2,999 \n286\t △ \\triangle \n287\t × \\times \n288\t △ \\triangle \n289\t △ \\triangle \n290\t\n291\t T2A-EpicBench (Wang et al. 2025b ) \n292\t\n293\t 100 \n294\t × \\times \n295\t × \\times \n296\t △ \\triangle \n297\t × \\times \n298\t\n299\t AudioScape-TTA (Ours) \n300\t 2,258 \n301\t ✓ \n302\t ✓ \n303\t ✓ \n304\t ✓ \n305\t\n306\t Table 1: \n307\tComparison of existing TTA benchmarks.\n308\t Structured Schema indicates whether audio scenes are represented using\n309\tpredefined components (e.g., sound effects, background music, and speech) rather\n310\tthan only free-form captions.\n311\t Speech Content indicates whether synthesized speech is evaluated against\n312\tannotated linguistic targets, such as transcripts or specified utterances.\n313\t Semantic Diagnosis indicates whether benchmarks provide\n314\tannotation-grounded criteria for diagnosing specific semantic requirements.\n315\t ✓ \\checkmark denotes full support, while △ \\triangle denotes partial support,\n316\tsuch as event-level, segment-level, or attribute-level evaluation without a unified\n317\tsemantic rubric.\n318\t Complexity Aware indicates whether evaluation explicitly characterizes\n319\tsample complexity or analyzes performance across different complexity levels.\n320\t\n321\t Figure 2: Overview of AudioScape-TTA , a complexity-aware benchmark for text-driven omni-audio generation. The top panel illustrates the benchmark construction and evaluation pipeline. The bottom panel characterizes AudioScape-TTA in a complexity-aware manner by organizing samples within the proposed Density–Structure complexity space. \n322\t\n323\t Related Work \n324\t\n325\t Text-to-Audio Generation \n326\t\n327\t Text-to-audio (TTA) generation aims to synthesize realistic audio from\n328\tnatural-language descriptions. Early approaches, such as AudioGen  (Kreuk et al. 2022 ) ,\n329\tformulate TTA as autoregressive generation over discrete audio tokens,\n330\tdemonstrating the feasibility of large-scale audio synthesis. Recent progress\n331\thas been largely driven by diffusion-based models. AudioLDM  (Liu et al. 2023 ) \n332\tintroduces latent diffusion for audio generation, while subsequent approaches,\n333\tincluding Make-An-Audio  (Huang et al. 2023b ) , Make-An-Audio 2  (Huang et al. 2023a ) ,\n334\tAuffusion  (Xue et al. 2024 ) , and EZAudio  (Hai et al. 2025 ) , further\n335\timprove semantic alignment, temporal modeling, and generation efficiency.\n336\tMore recently, flow-based methods such as TangoFlux  (Hung et al. 2024 ) \n337\tand MeanAudio  (Li et al. 2026b ) achieve faster generation with competitive\n338\tquality. In addition, unified audio generation frameworks, including\n339\tFoley-Omni  (Tao et al. 2026 ) , Omni2Sound  (Dai et al. 2026 ) , and\n340\tAudioStory  (Guo et al. 2026 ) , extend TTA toward multimodal audio synthesis. \n341\t\n342\t Despite recent progress, TTA evaluation remains dominated by perceptual quality\n343\tand global text–audio alignment metrics, while fine-grained semantic\n344\trequirement satisfaction in structured soundscapes is largely unexplored.\n345\t\n346\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. \n347\t\n348\t Existing TTA benchmarks evaluate generation capabilities from different\n349\tperspectives. Early datasets such as AudioCaps  (Kim et al. 2019 ) and\n350\tClotho  (Drossos et al. 2020 ) provide large-scale audio-caption pairs and\n351\tmainly assess global audio–text semantic alignment. However, their free-form\n352\tcaptions do not explicitly represent structured soundscape compositions or\n353\tseparate heterogeneous audio components.\n354\tRecent benchmarks investigate more specific generation capabilities.\n355\tAudioCondition  (Guo et al. 2024 ) explores condition-oriented audio generation,\n356\twhile AudioTime  (Xie et al. 2025 ) and RiTTA  (He et al. 2025 ) focus on\n357\ttemporal control and event-level relational reasoning.\n358\tTTA-Bench  (Wang et al. 2026 ) provides a broader evaluation suite covering\n359\tdiverse prompt categories. AudioAtlas  (Wang et al. 2025a ) and\n360\tVinTAGe-Bench  (Kushwaha and Tian 2025 ) further extend evaluation toward\n361\trealistic movie- and video-oriented audio scenarios.\n362\tRecent work has also investigated automatic evaluation of TTA systems through\n363\tlearned evaluation metrics, preference-based benchmarks, and rubric-based\n364\tevaluators, such as AQAScore (Kuan et al. 2026 ) , T2A-EpicBench (Wang et al. 2025b ) , and\n365\tAnyAudio-Judge (Li et al. 2026a ) . These approaches primarily focus on improving evaluation\n366\tmodels or measuring overall instruction-following performance, rather than\n367\testablishing a standardized benchmark with structured semantic annotations.\n368\tIn contrast, AudioScape-TTA focuses on benchmark construction. It introduces a\n369\tstructured soundscape, derives standardized audio-grounded semantic rubrics from hierarchical\n370\tannotations, and provides a unified, complexity-aware evaluation protocol for\n371\tthe reproducible assessment of compositional text-to-audio generation. \n372\t\n373\t AudioScape-TTA \n374\t\n375\t Overview \n376\t\n377\t AudioScape-TTA is a structured benchmark and evaluation system designed to\n378\tassess whether generated audio faithfully satisfies complex textual\n379\tinstructions. Instead of relying only on global text–audio correspondence,\n380\tAudioScape-TTA represents realistic soundscapes through structured audio\n381\tcomponents and enables fine-grained semantic evaluation through\n382\trequirement-level verification. \n383\t\n384\t As illustrated in Figure  2 , AudioScape-TTA consists of two main components:\n385\t(1) a structured soundscape benchmark that organizes audio scenes into explicit audio components and characterizes sample complexity through event density and structural complexity; and\n386\t(2) an audio-grounded rubrics-based evaluation system that converts structured soundscape descriptions into fine-grained semantic rubrics for assessing generated audio.\n387\tTogether, these components enable interpretable analysis of TTA systems beyond global text–audio similarity metrics. \n388\t\n389\t Structured Soundscape Benchmark \n390\t\n391\t Existing TTA benchmarks mainly provide caption-level descriptions, which are insufficient for analyzing realistic soundscapes containing multiple interacting audio components. To address this limitation, AudioScape-TTA introduces a structured representation that organizes audio scenes according to their semantic composition, including environmental context, background music, sound effects, and speech. \n392\t\n393\t Data Collection and Annotation. \n394\tAudioScape-TTA is constructed from real-world audio scenes extracted from movie and television productions. We first segment the original audio tracks into short clips and perform keyword-based retrieval to identify candidate samples covering diverse acoustic events and soundscape compositions. \n395\t\n396\t For each candidate clip, we adopt a semi-automatic annotation pipeline in which an LLM is used to assist the initial drafting of structured descriptions and component annotations. All annotations are subsequently reviewed and refined by trained annotators, who verify semantic correctness, revise inaccurate descriptions, refine annotations for scene context, sound effects, background music, and speech, and remove noisy or ambiguous samples to ensure annotation quality and consistency.\n397\tThe final benchmark is obtained through a three-stage pipeline consisting of candidate retrieval, LLM-assisted annotation, and human quality assurance, where human verification serves as the final authority for all benchmark annotations. \n398\t\n399\t Structured Soundscape Representation. \n400\tEach sample in AudioScape-TTA is represented as a structured soundscape\n401\tdescription consisting of a scene context and modality-specific audio components:\n402\t1) Scene : the overall acoustic environment or contextual setting of the audio clip;\n403\t2) Background Music (BGM) : musical elements such as instruments, style, and mood;\n404\t3) Sound Effects (SFX) : individual acoustic events and environmental actions;\n405\t4) Speech : spoken content and speaker-related information. \n406\t\n407\t The scene component provides high-level contextual information for describing realistic audio environments, while SFX, BGM, and speech define the explicit audio components evaluated in our semantic verification framework. This representation captures the compositional nature of realistic soundscapes and enables analysis beyond isolated event generation.\n408\tHere, composition refers to the clip-level organization and co-occurrence of predefined audio components. This representation focuses on semantic composition among soundscape components rather than explicitly modeling fine-grained temporal ordering or causal interactions among individual events. \n409\t\n410\t Complexity-aware Characterization. \n411\tTo enable complexity-aware evaluation, AudioScape-TTA characterizes each sample using two complementary dimensions: event density and structural complexity . Event density measures the amount of acoustic content in a clip, whereas structural complexity characterizes the diversity and clip-level composition of active audio categories. Together, these two dimensions capture complementary aspects of soundscape complexity, enabling systematic analysis of model robustness from simple scenes to dense and compositionally complex soundscapes.\n412\tBased on the normalized combination of these two dimensions, we partition samples into three complexity levels: Easy , Medium , and Hard . Figure  2 summarizes the distributions of event density and structural complexity, while the complete definitions of the two measures and the complexity partition strategy are provided in supplementary material. \n413\t\n414\t Data Statistics. \n415\tAs summarized in Table  3 , AudioScape-TTA contains\n416\t2,258 audio-text instances with 25,707 fine-grained semantic rubrics.\n417\tEach audio has an average duration of 9.95 seconds, and each textual\n418\tannotation contains 32.45 words on average. Each sample includes\n419\tapproximately 4 annotated semantic events, enabling fine-grained evaluation\n420\tof event realization, attribute fidelity, and speech-related requirements.\n421\tIn particular, speech annotations are provided for 358 clips, including\n422\ttarget-utterance evaluation and speech-attribute evaluation covering\n423\tspeaker and linguistic characteristics. Detailed statistics of modality\n424\tdistribution, event categories, and annotation characteristics are provided\n425\tin supplementary material. \n426\t\n427\t Model \n428\t Year \n429\t Params \n430\t Overall \n431\t Event Presence \n432\t Event Attribute \n433\t Speech Content \n434\t Text-Audio \n435\t\n436\t SR (%) ↑ \\uparrow \n437\t Conf. \n438\t SR (%) ↑ \\uparrow \n439\t Conf. \n440\t SR (%) ↑ \\uparrow \n441\t Conf. \n442\t SCCA@0.60 ↑ \\uparrow \n443\t 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS ↑ \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}}\\uparrow \n444\t\n445\t AudioLDM 2 \n446\t 2023 \n447\t 1.5B \n448\t 66.92 \n449\t 93.85 \n450\t 68.43 \n451\t 94.05 \n452\t 66.57 \n453\t 93.71 \n454\t 0.00 \n455\t 0.4193 \n456\t\n457\t Make-An-Audio 2 \n458\t 2023 \n459\t 937M \n460\t 61.33 \n461\t 92.99 \n462\t 61.48 \n463\t 93.26 \n464\t 61.89 \n465\t 92.80 \n466\t 0.00 \n467\t 0.3321 \n468\t\n469\t AudioGen \n470\t 2023 \n471\t 1.5B \n472\t 66.53 \n473\t 93.62 \n474\t 68.40 \n475\t 93.99 \n476\t 65.92 \n477\t 93.35 \n478\t 0.00 \n479\t 0.3664 \n480\t\n481\t Tango 2 \n482\t 2024 \n483\t 866M \n484\t 76.82 \n485\t 93.33 \n486\t 80.84 \n487\t 93.97 \n488\t 74.78 \n489\t 92.88 \n490\t 0.00 \n491\t 0.4145 \n492\t\n493\t TangoFlux \n494\t 2024 \n495\t 515M \n496\t 76.77 \n497\t 94.27 \n498\t 80.55 \n499\t 94.56 \n500\t 74.89 \n501\t 94.07 \n502\t 0.00 \n503\t 0.4736 \n504\t\n505\t EzAudio \n506\t 2025 \n507\t 875M \n508\t 78.20 \n509\t 94.14 \n510\t 80.83 \n511\t 94.38 \n512\t 77.17 \n513\t 93.97 \n514\t 0.00 \n515\t 0.4713 \n516\t\n517\t MAGNeT \n518\t 2024 \n519\t 1.5B \n520\t 53.39 \n521\t 92.44 \n522\t 49.89 \n523\t 92.38 \n524\t 56.50 \n525\t 92.48 \n526\t 0.00 \n527\t 0.2827 \n528\t\n529\t Stable Audio Open \n530\t 2025 \n531\t 1.1B \n532\t 65.95 \n533\t 93.59 \n534\t 67.57 \n535\t 93.98 \n536\t 65.51 \n537\t 93.32 \n538\t 0.00 \n539\t 0.3480 \n540\t\n541\t MMAudio \n542\t 2025 \n543\t 1.03B \n544\t 64.73 \n545\t 94.15 \n546\t 67.80 \n547\t 94.52 \n548\t 63.24 \n549\t 93.89 \n550\t 0.00 \n551\t 0.5169 \n552\t\n553\t Foley-Omni \n554\t 2026 \n555\t 5.5B \n556\t 79.62 \n557\t 94.05 \n558\t 81.74 \n559\t 94.66 \n560\t 78.34 \n561\t 93.61 \n562\t 57.06 \n563\t 0.4378 \n564\t\n565\t Omni2Sound \n566\t 2026 \n567\t 1.33B \n568\t 77.59 \n569\t 94.47 \n570\t 79.98 \n571\t 94.86 \n572\t 76.72 \n573\t 94.20 \n574\t 0.00 \n575\t 0.4370 \n576\t\n577\t AudioStory \n578\t 2026 \n579\t 4.36B \n580\t 69.03 \n581\t 93.04 \n582\t 70.09 \n583\t 93.36 \n584\t 69.02 \n585\t 92.80 \n586\t 0.00 \n587\t 0.3989 \n588\t\n589\t Dasheng AudioGen \n590\t 2026 \n591\t 2.19B \n592\t 74.50 \n593\t 94.17 \n594\t 74.86 \n595\t 94.27 \n596\t 74.21 \n597\t 94.09 \n598\t 77.30 \n599\t 0.4139 \n600\t\n601\t Table 2: \n602\tFine-grained semantic evaluation results on AudioScape-TTA.\n603\tBest and second-best results are highlighted in bold and\n604\t underline , respectively.\n605\t\n606\t Statistics \n607\t Number \n608\t\n609\t Total Samples \n610\t 2,258 \n611\t\n612\t Total Semantic Rubrics \n613\t 25,707 \n614\t\n615\t Avg. Audio Duration \n616\t 9.95 sec \n617\t\n618\t Avg. Annotation Length \n619\t 32.45 words \n620\t\n621\t Avg. Semantic Rubrics \n622\t 11.39 \n623\t\n624\t Avg. Events \n625\t 4 \n626\t\n627\t Speech-related Statistics \n628\t\n629\t Speech-annotated Clips \n630\t 358 (15.85%) \n631\t\n632\t Clips with Target Utterances \n633\t 83 \n634\t\n635\t Speech-content Rubrics \n636\t 163 \n637\t\n638\t Speech-attribute Rubrics \n639\t 931 \n640\t\n641\t Table 3: \n642\tKey statistics of the AudioScape-TTA benchmark.\n643\t\n644\t Rubric-based Audio-grounded Evaluation \n645\t\n646\t Given a structured soundscape description, the goal of evaluation is to determine whether each predefined semantic requirement is satisfied by the generated audio. To this end, we propose a rubric-based audio-grounded evaluation framework that converts each structured annotation into a fixed set of semantic verification rubrics and evaluates them through audio-language understanding. As illustrated in Figure  2 , the framework consists of three stages: (1) structured rubric construction, (2) audio-grounded verification, and (3) hierarchical score aggregation. \n647\t\n648\t Structured Rubric Construction. \n649\tGiven a structured description, we employ Qwen3.5-27B  (Qwen Team 2026 ) to extract a hierarchical semantic representation: \n650\t\n651\t Sample → Modality → Event → Attribute . \\text{Sample}\\rightarrow\\text{Modality}\\rightarrow\\text{Event}\\rightarrow\\text{Attribute}. \n652\t\n653\t Based on this hierarchy, we construct a fixed set of binary semantic rubrics during benchmark construction. Each rubric defines a fixed semantic verification criterion and is shared across all evaluated models, ensuring consistent and auditable comparison:\n654\t(1) Event Presence , which verifies whether a required sound event is generated;\n655\t(2) Event Attribute , which verifies whether the generated event satisfies the specified acoustic properties;\n656\t(3) Speech Content , which verifies whether synthesized speech preserves the intended linguistic content. \n657\t\n658\t Audio-grounded Verification. \n659\tEach semantic rubric is evaluated using a verifier according to its semantic type. Event-presence and event-attribute rubrics are verified by Qwen3-Omni-Instruct  (Xu et al. 2025 ) , while speech-content rubrics are evaluated by Qwen3-ASR  (Shi et al. 2026 ) through transcript matching with the annotated target utterance. \n660\t\n661\t Hierarchical Score Aggregation. \n662\tEach fixed semantic rubric is independently verified and contributes equally to the final benchmark score, which is computed as the micro-average satisfaction rate over all rubrics.\n663\tBeyond the overall score, AudioScape-TTA provides hierarchical evaluation from three complementary perspectives. Rubrics-based evaluation measures semantic instruction following across event presence, event attributes, and speech content. Modality-level evaluation analyzes performance on sound effects, background music, and speech. Complexity-aware evaluation further assesses model robustness across Easy, Medium, and Hard soundscapes characterized by different levels of event density and structural complexity. Together, these analyses provide interpretable diagnosis of semantic instruction following beyond a single aggregate score. Detailed metric definitions are provided in supplementary material. \n664\t\n665\t Experiments \n666\t\n667\t Experimental Setup \n668\t\n669\t Evaluated Models. \n670\tWe evaluate 13 open-source TTA models on AudioScape-TTA, covering diverse generation paradigms. The evaluated models include AudioLDM2  (Liu et al. 2023 ) , Make-An-Audio 2  (Huang et al. 2023a ) , MMAudio  (Cheng et al. 2025 ) , Tango 2  (Majumder et al. 2024 ) , TangoFlux  (Hung et al. 2024 ) , EzAudio  (Hai et al. 2025 ) , MAGNeT  (Ziv et al. 2024 ) , AudioGen  (Kreuk et al. 2022 ) , Stable Audio Open  (Evans et al. 2025 ) , Foley-Omni  (Tao et al. 2026 ) , Omni2Sound  (Dai et al. 2026 ) , AudioStory  (Guo et al. 2026 ) , and Dasheng AudioGen  (Mei et al. 2026 ) .\n671\tDetailed model architectures and generation paradigms are summarized in Table  7 in the supplementary material. \n672\t\n673\t Evaluation Protocol. \n674\tWe evaluate generated audio from two complementary perspectives:\n675\tfine-grained requirement satisfaction and global generation quality.\n676\tFor fine-grained evaluation, we report requirement satisfaction rates\n677\tover overall, modality-specific, complexity-level, and QA-type subsets.\n678\tEvent-presence and event-attribute requirements are evaluated by\n679\tQwen3-Omni-Instruct  (Xu et al. 2025 ) using binary verification QA,\n680\twhere each rubric checks whether a specified target requirement is realized\n681\tin the generated audio. Speech-content requirements are evaluated by\n682\tQwen3-ASR  (Shi et al. 2026 ) based on transcript matching. \n683\t\n684\t Since all semantic rubrics in the benchmark are formulated as positive\n685\trequirements, the resulting score measures the proportion of requested\n686\trequirements successfully satisfied by the generated audio rather than\n687\tgeneral semantic satisfaction rate or hallucination-free generation.\n688\tAccordingly, we denote a metric as Satisfaction Rate (SR).\n689\tFor speech-content requirements, we introduce Speech Content Coverage\n690\tSatisfaction Rate at threshold 0.60 (SCCA@0.60) to convert transcript matching into binary requirement satisfaction.\n691\tWord Error Rate (WER) and Character Error Rate (CER) are further\n692\treported in the supplementary material as complementary transcription fidelity diagnostics,\n693\tbut are not used for the primary semantic evaluation because many TTA systems\n694\tdo not explicitly generate speech content, resulting in degenerate ASR-based\n695\tscores.\n696\t\n697\t For global audio quality assessment, we include Audiobox-Aesthetic\n698\tmetrics, including Content Enjoyment (CE), Content Usefulness (CU),\n699\tProduction Complexity (PC), and Production Quality (PQ)  (Tjandra et al. 2025 ) ;\n700\tText-audio alignment score\n701\t( 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} )  (Elizalde et al. 2024 ) ;\n702\tand distribution-based metrics including Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD),\n703\tKullback–Leibler (KL) divergence, and Inception Score (ISC).\n704\tDetailed metric definitions and implementation details are provided in\n705\tsupplementary material. \n706\t\n707\t Figure 3: User study results on the AudioScape-TTA benchmark. Markers indicate mean human ratings for event completeness, attribute satisfaction rate, and audio quality, while error bars denote standard deviations. \n708\t\n709\t Fine-grained Semantic Evaluation \n710\t\n711\t Overall Results. \n712\tTable  2 summarizes the fine-grained semantic evaluation results.\n713\tAmong all evaluated models, Foley-Omni achieves the best overall performance,\n714\tobtaining 79.62% SR and ranking first on both event-presence\n715\t(81.74%) and event-attribute (78.34%) evaluation.\n716\tTwo observations are noteworthy. First, event-attribute satisfaction rate consistently\n717\tlags behind event-presence satisfaction rate, indicating that accurately generating a\n718\ttarget event is substantially easier than faithfully controlling its acoustic\n719\tattributes. Second, speech-content generation remains a major challenge.\n720\tExcept for Foley-Omni and Dasheng AudioGen, all evaluated models achieve\n721\t0% SCCA, revealing that current open-source TTA systems largely fail to\n722\tpreserve linguistic content despite producing speech-like audio. \n723\t\n724\t Modality-specific Analysis. \n725\tFigure  1 (a) reveals clear modality-specific\n726\tdifferences among current TTA systems. Foley-Omni achieves the strongest\n727\tperformance on sound effects, while EzAudio performs best on music. Speech is\n728\tconsiderably more challenging than the other modalities, with Dasheng AudioGen\n729\tbeing the only model achieving competitive speech-content satisfaction rate.\n730\tPerformance further degrades on multi-modal compositions that jointly involve\n731\tsound effects, music, and speech (Table  9 in the\n732\tsupplementary material), suggesting that modeling interactions among audio\n733\tcomponents remains a key challenge for current TTA systems. Foley-Omni and\n734\tDasheng AudioGen show the strongest robustness under these compositional\n735\tsettings. \n736\t\n737\t Complexity-aware Analysis. \n738\tFigure  1 (b) shows that the semantic satisfaction rate consistently\n739\tdegrades as complexity increases. While several models perform\n740\tcompetitively on easy subsets, their performance drops substantially on medium\n741\tand hard subsets. Foley-Omni exhibits strong robustness, particularly on\n742\tthe hard subset, indicating superior semantic grounding under dense\n743\tcompositional soundscapes. \n744\t\n745\t Comparison with Conventional Evaluation Metrics \n746\t\n747\t Table  2 additionally reports 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} as\n748\ta conventional global text–audio alignment metric for comparison. The ranking\n749\tunder CLAP-based similarity is not consistent with fine-grained\n750\trubrics-based semantic satisfaction. For example, MMAudio achieves the highest\n751\t 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} score (0.5169) but obtains only 64.73% semantic\n752\trequirement satisfaction rate, indicating that strong global text–audio\n753\talignment does not necessarily guarantee faithful realization of individual\n754\tsemantic requirements. In contrast, Foley-Omni achieves the highest semantic\n755\tsatisfaction rate while ranking lower under CLAP-based alignment.\n756\tThese results suggest that global embedding-based metrics and\n757\trubrics-based evaluation capture complementary aspects of TTA generation:\n758\tCLAP measures overall text–audio correspondence, whereas rubrics-based\n759\tevaluation directly verifies whether individual prompt-specified requirements\n760\tare satisfied. \n761\t\n762\t We further report perceptual quality and distribution-based metrics, including\n763\tAudiobox-Aesthetic scores, FAD, KL divergence, and ISC, in the supplementary\n764\tmaterial. \n765\t\n766\t User Study \n767\t\n768\t To obtain human judgments for validating metric reliability, we conduct a user\n769\tstudy on AudioScape-TTA. We randomly select 10 prompts covering diverse\n770\tmodality compositions and complexity levels, and collect generated audio from\n771\tall 13 evaluated models, resulting in 130 samples. Each sample is rated by 26\n772\tparticipants on three dimensions: event completeness ,\n773\t attribute satisfaction rate , and audio quality . Event completeness\n774\tmeasures whether required events are generated, attribute satisfaction rate evaluates\n775\twhether fine-grained acoustic properties are preserved, and audio quality\n776\tassesses perceptual naturalness and generation artifacts. Participants rate each dimension using a 5-point Likert scale. \n777\t\n778\t As shown in Figure  3 , human judgments closely agree with\n779\tthe fine-grained QA evaluation. Foley-Omni achieves the highest scores in event\n780\tcompleteness and attribute satisfaction rate, consistent with its leading semantic\n781\tfollowing performance. Dasheng AudioGen obtains the highest audio quality\n782\tscore, indicating stronger perceptual quality among the evaluated models. \n783\t\n784\t Human Alignment of Evaluation Metrics \n785\t\n786\t We quantify the agreement between human judgments and automatic metrics using model-level Spearman correlation across the 13 evaluated TTA models. Human evaluation provides ratings for event completeness, attribute satisfaction, and audio quality. To summarize semantic instruction following into a single target, we define a composite human semantic score (Semantic † ) as the equal-weight average of event completeness and attribute satisfaction. Audio quality is excluded because it measures perceptual preference rather than semantic correctness. \n787\t\n788\t Table  4 shows that the our rubric-based metrics achieve strong alignment with corresponding human judgments. Event SR exhibits a strong correlation with human event-completeness ratings, while Attribute SR closely matches human attribute-satisfaction ratings. The aggregated Overall SR achieves the highest correlation with the composite human semantic score, substantially outperforming 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} , which measures global text–audio similarity. These results demonstrate that rubric-based evaluation better captures human-perceived semantic instruction following than global embedding-based similarity metrics in our evaluation setting. \n789\t\n790\t Human Judgment \n791\t Metric \n792\t ρ \\rho \n793\t p Holm p_{\\mathrm{Holm}} \n794\t\n795\t Completeness \n796\t Event SR \n797\t 0.743 \n798\t 0.0072 \n799\t\n800\t Attribute \n801\t Attribute SR \n802\t 0.825 \n803\t 0.0003 \n804\t\n805\t Semantic † \n806\t Overall SR \n807\t 0.879 \n808\t 0.0015 \n809\t\n810\t 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} \n811\t 0.312 \n812\t 0.0739 \n813\t\n814\t Table 4: \n815\tModel-level Spearman correlations between automatic evaluation metrics and human judgments across 13 TTA models using the same samples as the user study.\n816\tAll two-sided p p values are Holm-adjusted over the four correlation tests.\n817\tBold indicates the highest correlation coefficient.\n818\tSemantic † denotes the equal-weight average of event completeness and attribute satisfaction.\n819\t\n820\t Discussion \n821\t\n822\t Our analysis reveals several key challenges in current text-to-audio\n823\tgeneration.\n824\tFirst, semantic event recognition and fine-grained acoustic\n825\tcontrol remain fundamentally different capabilities. While current models can\n826\toften generate the target sound events, reproducing the associated attributes\n827\tand detailed acoustic characteristics remains challenging, suggesting that\n828\tfuture TTA models require stronger compositional and attribute-aware\n829\tgeneration mechanisms.\n830\tSecond, speech generation remains a distinct challenge compared with other\n831\taudio modalities. Preserving linguistic content, speaker identity, and\n832\tacoustic realism simultaneously requires modeling multiple aspects of speech,\n833\twhich is not yet reliably achieved by unified TTA systems.\n834\tThird, current models struggle with highly compositional soundscapes involving\n835\tmultiple events and interacting modalities. The degradation under increasing\n836\tcomplexity suggests that existing systems are still primarily optimized for\n837\tindividual sound generation rather than structured audio scene composition.\n838\tFinally, our human correlation analysis highlights the importance of\n839\tfine-grained evaluation for future TTA research. Global metrics remain useful\n840\tfor assessing perceptual quality and coarse similarity, but they provide\n841\tlimited insight into specific semantic failures. Structured evaluation enables\n842\tmore interpretable diagnosis of model capabilities and facilitates future\n843\tprogress toward controllable audio generation. \n844\t\n845\t Conclusion \n846\t\n847\t In this work, we introduced AudioScape-TTA , a structured and\n848\tcomplexity-aware benchmark and evaluation framework for fine-grained\n849\ttext-to-audio generation. By representing soundscapes through\n850\tmodality-aware semantic structures and evaluating generation results through\n851\trubric-based verification, AudioScape-TTA enables interpretable\n852\tanalysis beyond conventional global similarity metrics. Extensive experiments\n853\ton 13 open-source TTA models reveal persistent limitations in fine-grained\n854\tattribute realization, speech-content preservation, and compositional\n855\tsoundscape generation. Human validation further demonstrates that our\n856\tevaluation framework aligns well with human semantic judgments, highlighting\n857\tthe importance of requirement-level evaluation for assessing TTA instruction\n858\tfollowing.\n859\tWe hope AudioScape-TTA can facilitate the development of more controllable and\n860\tsemantically faithful audio generation systems. Future extensions may explore\n861\tricher soundscape representations with temporal, spatial, and causal relations,\n862\tas well as more advanced evaluation protocols for complex audio interactions\n863\tand long-form generation scenarios. \n864\t\n865\t References \n866\t\n867\t H. K. Cheng, M. Ishii, A. Hayakawa, T. Shibuya, A. Schwing, and Y. Mitsufuji (2025) \n868\t Mmaudio: taming multimodal joint training for high-quality video-to-audio synthesis .\n869\t\n870\t In Proceedings of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference ,\n871\t\n872\t pp. 28901–28911 .\n873\t\n874\t Cited by: Experimental Setup .\n875\t\n876\t Y. Dai, Z. Chen, Y. Jiang, Q. Ke, J. Cai, and J. Zhu (2026) \n877\t Omni2sound: towards unified video-text-to-audio generation .\n878\t\n879\t In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition ,\n880\t\n881\t pp. 1661–1671 .\n882\t\n883\t Cited by: Text-to-Audio Generation ,\n884\t Experimental Setup .\n885\t\n886\t K. Drossos, S. Lipping, and T. Virtanen (2020) \n887\t Clotho: an audio captioning dataset .\n888\t\n889\t In ICASSP 2020-2020 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) ,\n890\t\n891\t pp. 736–740 .\n892\t\n893\t Cited by: Table 1 ,\n894\t Introduction ,\n895\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. .\n896\t\n897\t B. Elizalde, S. Deshmukh, and H. Wang (2024) \n898\t Natural language supervision for general-purpose audio representations .\n899\t\n900\t In ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) ,\n901\t\n902\t pp. 336–340 .\n903\t\n904\t Cited by: Experimental Setup