of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence ,\n1112\t\n1113\t Vol. 40 , pp. 33512–33520 .\n1114\t\n1115\t Cited by: Table 1 ,\n1116\t Introduction ,\n1117\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. .\n1118\t\n1119\t Z. Wang, K. Lei, C. Zhu, J. Huang, S. Zhou, L. Liu, X. Cheng, S. Ji, Z. Ye, T. Jin, et al. (2025b) \n1120\t T2A-feedback: improving basic capabilities of text-to-audio generation via fine-grained ai feedback .\n1121\t\n1122\t In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers) ,\n1123\t\n1124\t pp. 23535–23547 .\n1125\t\n1126\t Cited by: Table 1 ,\n1127\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. .\n1128\t\n1129\t Y. Wu, K. Chen, T. Zhang, Y. Hui, T. Berg-Kirkpatrick, and S. Dubnov (2023) \n1130\t Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with feature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation .\n1131\t\n1132\t In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) ,\n1133\t\n1134\t pp. 1–5 .\n1135\t\n1136\t Cited by: Introduction .\n1137\t\n1138\t Z. Xie, X. Xu, Z. Wu, and M. Wu (2025) \n1139\t Audiotime: a temporally-aligned audio-text benchmark dataset .\n1140\t\n1141\t In ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) ,\n1142\t\n1143\t pp. 1–5 .\n1144\t\n1145\t Cited by: Table 1 ,\n1146\t Introduction ,\n1147\t Benchmarks and Evaluation of TTA Generation. .\n1148\t\n1149\t J. Xu, Z. Guo, H. Hu, Y. Chu, X. Wang, J. He, Y. Wang, X. Shi, T. He, X. Zhu, et al. (2025) \n1150\t Qwen3-omni technical report .\n1151\t\n1152\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.17765 .\n1153\t\n1154\t Cited by: Rubric-based Audio-grounded Evaluation ,\n1155\t Experimental Setup .\n1156\t\n1157\t J. Xue, Y. Deng, Y. Gao, and Y. Li (2024) \n1158\t Auffusion: leveraging the power of diffusion and large language models for text-to-audio generation .\n1159\t\n1160\t IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 32 , pp. 4700–4712 .\n1161\t\n1162\t Cited by: Text-to-Audio Generation .\n1163\t\n1164\t D. Yang, J. Tian, X. Tan, R. Huang, S. Liu, X. Chang, J. Shi, S. Zhao, J. Bian, Z. Zhao, et al. (2023) \n1165\t Uniaudio: an audio foundation model toward universal audio generation .\n1166\t\n1167\t arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.00704 .\n1168\t\n1169\t Cited by: Introduction .\n1170\t\n1171\t A. Ziv, I. Gat, G. Le Lan, T. Remez, F. Kreuk, J. Copet, A. Défossez, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi (2024) \n1172\t Masked audio generation using a single non-autoregressive transformer .\n1173\t\n1174\t In International Conference on Learning Representations ,\n1175\t\n1176\t Vol. 2024 , pp. 43249–43268 .\n1177\t\n1178\t Cited by: Experimental Setup .\n1179\t\n1180\t Additional Dataset Details \n1181\t\n1182\t Annotation Pipeline \n1183\t\n1184\t The construction of AudioScape-TTA follows a three-stage annotation pipeline. \n1185\t\n1186\t First, candidate audio clips are retrieved using keyword-based filtering from a large-scale video repository. Second, Gemini-2.5-Pro is used to generate initial descriptions and\n1187\tcandidate structured annotations, including modality-level events and\n1188\tattributes. Third, human annotators verify and refine these annotations,\n1189\tcorrect semantic errors, and remove ambiguous samples. \n1190\t\n1191\t This procedure ensures both annotation scalability and semantic reliability. \n1192\t\n1193\t Complexity Score Definition \n1194\t\n1195\t We characterize sample complexity using two complementary dimensions: event density and structural complexity . While event density measures the amount of acoustic content contained in a clip, structural complexity characterizes the diversity and clip-level composition of active audio categories. Together, they capture two complementary aspects of generation complexity. \n1196\t\n1197\t Event Density. \n1198\t\n1199\t Event density measures how much acoustic content is contained in a soundscape. It is defined as \n1200\t\n1201\t Density = N SFX + N Speech + N BGM , \\text{Density}=N_{\\mathrm{SFX}}+N_{\\mathrm{Speech}}+N_{\\mathrm{BGM}}, \n1202\t\n1203\t (1) \n1204\t\n1205\t where N SFX N_{\\mathrm{SFX}} denotes the number of annotated SFX event instances in a clip, with repeated occurrences counted separately. In contrast, N Speech ∈ { 0 , 1 } N_{\\mathrm{Speech}}\\in\\{0,1\\} and N BGM ∈ { 0 , 1 } N_{\\mathrm{BGM}}\\in\\{0,1\\} are binary indicators denoting the presence of speech and background music, respectively. \n1206\t\n1207\t This asymmetric definition reflects the different temporal characteristics of the three audio categories. SFX typically consists of discrete and countable event instances, whereas speech usually forms a continuous dialogue layer and BGM serves as a sustained background layer. Counting individual speech utterances or music segments would depend heavily on annotation granularity and could artificially inflate the density score. We therefore count SFX at the event-instance level while representing speech and BGM solely by their clip-level presence. Note that density does not represent physical loudness or duration; it only measures the number of annotated semantic components. \n1208\t\n1209\t Complexity \n1210\t\n1211\t Annotation \n1212\t\n1213\t Semantic Rubrics \n1214\t\n1215\t Easy \n1216\t\n1217\t [Scene] Outdoor water source environment;\n1218\t[SFX] Gentle continuous gurgling and trickling of flowing water;\n1219\t[SFX] Water pouring out of the spout. \n1220\t\n1221\t Event presence: 1. Is there an outdoor water source environment?\n1222\t2. Is there flowing water? 3. Is there water pouring out of the spout? \n1223\t Event attributes: 1. Is the sound of flowing water characterized by gurgling and trickling?\n1224\t2. Is the sound of flowing water gentle and continuous? \n1225\t\n1226\t Medium \n1227\t\n1228\t [Scene] Indoor cafe/kitchen environment;\n1229\t[BGM] Slow gentle sentimental piano music;\n1230\t[SFX] Grinding/blending machinery sound;\n1231\t[SFX] Subtle clinks and food preparation sounds;\n1232\t[SFX] Bowl being placed on counter. \n1233\t\n1234\t Event presence: 1. Is there an indoor cafe/kitchen environment present? 2. Is there a grinding or blending machinery sound present? 3. Are there subtle clinks and food-preparation sounds? 4. Is there a bowl being placed on counter present? 5. Is there piano music present? \n1235\t Event attributes: 1. Is the sound of food preparation characterized by subtle clinks? 2. Is the mood of the piano music slow, gentle, and sentimental? 3. Is the instrument of the slow gentle sentimental music a piano? \n1236\t\n1237\t Hard \n1238\t\n1239\t [Scene] Indoor closed environment;\n1240\t[BGM] Tense atmospheric dramatic score with ominous synthesized tones;\n1241\t[SFX] Male speaking; Thunder and lightning; Baby crying;\n1242\t[Speaker A, elderly male, hoarse voice]: “Tell the story. He said he could hear that baby cry just as plain…” \n1243\t\n1244\t Event presence: 1. Is there an indoor closed environment?\n1245\t2. Is there a male speaking?\n1246\t3. Is there thunder and lightning?\n1247\t4. Is there a baby crying?\n1248\t5. Are ominous synthesized tones present?\n1249\t\n1250\t Speech content: Does the speech include the phrase ’tell the story. He said he could hear that baby cry just as plain. And um…? \n1251\t Event attributes: 1. Is the speaker male?\n1252\t2. Is the speaker elderly?\n1253\t3. Is the speaker speaking in hoarse voice?\n1254\t4. Is the source of the crying referred to as a baby?\n1255\t5. Is the action or content of the crying described as crying?\n1256\t6. Is the genre of the score described as atmospheric dramatic?\n1257\t7. Is the mood of the score described as tense?\n1258\t8. Is the instrument of the score described as synthesized tones? \n1259\t\n1260\t Table 5: Examples of structured annotation and semantic rubrics across different complexity levels. \n1261\t\n1262\t Figure 4: Statistics of the proposed AudioScape-TTA benchmark. \n1263\t(a) Density distribution reflects the combination of discrete SFX-instance\n1264\tcounts and clip-level presence indicators for speech and BGM.\n1265\t(b) Structure score distribution characterizes the complexity and organization of multi-event soundscapes.\n1266\t(c) and (d) summarize speaker number and speech language distributions, highlighting the diversity of speech-related scenarios.\n1267\tThese statistics demonstrate the multimodal diversity and compositional complexity of the benchmark. \n1268\t\n1269\t Structural Complexity. \n1270\t\n1271\t Structural complexity measures how diverse the composition of a soundscape is, independent of the number of event instances. It is defined as \n1272\t\n1273\t Structure_Score = S modality + S cooccur , \\text{Structure\\_Score}=S_{\\mathrm{modality}}+S_{\\mathrm{cooccur}}, \n1274\t\n1275\t (2) \n1276\t\n1277\t where S modality S_{\\mathrm{modality}} denotes the number of active audio categories among\n1278\t Speech , SFX , and BGM . We treat speech as a distinct\n1279\tmodality because, unlike non-speech audio components, speech generation requires\n1280\tnot only acoustic realization but also preservation of linguistic content and\n1281\tspeaker-related characteristics. \n1282\t\n1283\t The clip-level co-occurrence term S cooccur ∈ { 0 , 1 } S_{\\mathrm{cooccur}}\\in\\{0,1\\} is defined as \n1284\t\n1285\t S cooccur = 𝕀 ​ [ Speech ∧ ( SFX ∨ BGM ) ] , S_{\\mathrm{cooccur}}=\\mathbb{I}\\left[\\mathrm{Speech}\\land(\\mathrm{SFX}\\lor\\mathrm{BGM})\\right], \n1286\t\n1287\t (3) \n1288\t\n1289\t where 𝕀 ​ [ ⋅ ] \\mathbb{I}[\\cdot] is the indicator function. It equals 1 1 when speech\n1290\tco-occurs with at least one non-speech audio category (SFX or BGM), and 0 \n1291\totherwise. Therefore, Structure_Score ranges from 0 to 4, where larger values\n1292\tindicate more diverse modality combinations. \n1293\t\n1294\t Unlike event density, structural complexity does not depend on the number of SFX\n1295\tinstances. Instead, it captures the diversity of active audio categories and\n1296\twhether heterogeneous audio components must be generated jointly. We explicitly\n1297\tconsider speech-related co-occurrence because unified speech-aware audio\n1298\tgeneration introduces additional requirements beyond non-speech sound synthesis,\n1299\tincluding linguistic content preservation, speaker identity modeling, and\n1300\tcoordination between vocal and non-vocal components. Therefore, soundscapes\n1301\tcontaining speech together with SFX or BGM typically require joint modeling of\n1302\tsemantic, acoustic, and linguistic factors. \n1303\t\n1304\t The proposed structural complexity is designed as a practical benchmark-oriented\n1305\tcomplexity heuristic rather than a claim that speech is universally more complex\n1306\tthan other audio categories. The co-occurrence term characterizes category-level\n1307\tcomposition only; it does not explicitly model temporal overlap, event ordering,\n1308\tcausal relations, or semantic interactions among components.\n1309\tAccordingly, the proposed complexity characterization captures clip-level\n1310\taudio-category composition rather than explicit temporal overlap, event\n1311\tordering, causal relations, or semantic interactions among individual events.\n1312\tReferences to compositional difficulty in this paper should therefore be\n1313\tinterpreted under this benchmark-specific Density–Structure characterization. \n1314\t\n1315\t Complexity Score. \n1316\t\n1317\t Since event density and structural complexity are measured on different scales, we first normalize them using z-score normalization and then compute the overall complexity score as \n1318\t\n1319\t C = Z ​ ( Density ) + Z ​ ( Structure_Score ) , C=Z(\\text{Density})+Z(\\text{Structure\\_Score}), \n1320\t\n1321\t (4) \n1322\t\n1323\t where Z ​ ( ⋅ ) Z(\\cdot) denotes z-score normalization. \n1324\t\n1325\t Finally, samples are partitioned into three complexity levels according to \n1326\t\n1327\t { Easy , C ≤ − 1 , Medium , − 1 < C < 1 , Hard , C ≥ 1 . \\begin{cases}\\text{Easy},&C\\leq-1,\\\\\n1328\t\\text{Medium},&-1<C<1,\\\\\n1329\t\\text{Hard},&C\\geq 1.\\end{cases} \n1330\t\n1331\t The thresholds are selected based on standardized complexity scores to\n1332\tseparate low-, medium-, and high-complexity samples while maintaining\n1333\tsufficient samples in each subset.\n1334\tThis formulation enables complexity-aware evaluation by jointly considering both the amount of acoustic content and the compositional diversity of structured soundscapes. \n1335\t\n1336\t Complementarity Analysis. \n1337\t\n1338\t Across all 2,258 samples, event density and structural complexity exhibit only weak correlation (Pearson r = 0.221 r=0.221 ; Spearman ρ = 0.153 \\rho=0.153 ), indicating that the two metrics capture complementary aspects of audio-scene complexity. Specifically, event density measures the amount of acoustic content, whereas structural complexity characterizes the diversity and co-occurrence patterns of audio components. \n1339\t\n1340\t Subset \n1341\t # Samples \n1342\t Avg. SFX \n1343\t Speech (%) \n1344\t BGM (%) \n1345\t Multi-comp. (%) \n1346\t Avg. Rubrics \n1347\t\n1348\t Easy \n1349\t 732 \n1350\t 2.36 \n1351\t 0.00 \n1352\t 10.52 \n1353\t 0.00 \n1354\t 7.62 \n1355\t\n1356\t Medium \n1357\t 1,103 \n1358\t 3.91 \n1359\t 0.73 \n1360\t 25.84 \n1361\t 26.38 \n1362\t 11.92 \n1363\t\n1364\t Hard \n1365\t 423 \n1366\t 4.48 \n1367\t 56.03 \n1368\t 56.50 \n1369\t 85.11 \n1370\t 16.51 \n1371\t\n1372\t Table 6: \n1373\tComposition statistics of the Easy, Medium, and Hard subsets.\n1374\tAvg. SFX denotes the mean number of annotated SFX instances per clip.\n1375\tSpeech and BGM indicate the percentages of clips containing the corresponding\n1376\tmodality. Multi-comp. denotes the percentage of clips containing at least two\n1377\tmodalities among Speech, SFX, and BGM. Avg. Rubrics is the average number of\n1378\tQA rubrics per clip in the final released benchmark.\n1379\t\n1380\t Data Statistics \n1381\t\n1382\t To further demonstrate the diversity and complexity of the proposed AudioScape-TTA benchmark, we provide additional annotation examples and dataset statistics in the supplementary material.\n1383\tTable  5 presents representative samples with different complexity levels, where each audio scene is described through a hierarchical annotation scheme covering scene context, sound effects (SFX), background music (BGM), speaker attributes, and speech content.\n1384\tThe corresponding semantic rubrics further decompose these complex descriptions into fine-grained evaluation criteria, enabling detailed assessment of event presence, attributes, and speech semantics.\n1385\tAs the complexity increases, samples contain richer modality combinations, more discrete SFX instances, and more complex clip-level audio-category composition, ranging from simple isolated sound sources to challenging scenarios involving simultaneous environmental sounds, music, and speech. \n1386\t\n1387\t Moreover, Figure  4 summarizes the benchmark statistics from multiple perspectives.\n1388\tThe density and structure score distributions characterize the complexity of multi-event soundscapes, reflecting the number of co-occurring audio elements and their structural organization.\n1389\tThe speaker and language distributions further demonstrate the diversity of speech-related scenarios.\n1390\tTogether, these results highlight that AudioScape-TTA covers diverse audio modalities and challenging compositional sound environments, providing a comprehensive testbed for evaluating TTA generation models. \n1391\t\n1392\t Table  6 summarizes the composition of the Easy, Medium, and Hard subsets. As the difficulty level increases, audio scenes become progressively more complex, with more annotated SFX events, higher proportions of speech and background music, more multimodal compositions, and a larger number of QA rubrics per clip. These statistics confirm that the proposed difficulty partition reflects increasing compositional complexity rather than merely differences in sample count. \n1393\t\n1394\t Semantic Rubrics Construction Details \n1395\t\n1396\t We construct rubrics pairs from structured event-level annotations using a deterministic target-generation pipeline followed by natural-language rewriting. The semantic target of each rubrics pair is derived directly from the annotation, while the LLM is only used to rewrite the target into a concise listener-answerable yes/no question. Therefore, the LLM does not decide the answer or introduce new semantic targets. All rubrics pairs use the same two-choice format, A: Yes and B: No , and the reference answer is set to A because each question is generated from a positive annotated event or attribute. \n1397\t\n1398\t Rubrics Types. \n1399\t\n1400\t We generate three types of rubrics pairs: \n1401\t\n1402\t • \n1403\t\n1404\t Event presence : verifies whether a non-speech sound event, background music, or acoustic scene is present in the audio. \n1405\t\n1406\t • \n1407\t\n1408\t Event attribute : verifies whether a specific event-level acoustic attribute is realized, such as sound source, material, intensity, temporal pattern, spatial property, instrument, emotion, speaker identity, or language. \n1409\t\n1410\t • \n1411\t\n1412\t Speech content : verifies whether the spoken content of a speech event is preserved. \n1413\t\n1414\t Structured rubrics schema. \n1415\t\n1416\t Each QA rubrics pair stores both the natural-language question and its structured evidence: \n1417\t\n1418\t ⬇ \n1419\t\n1420\t 1 { \n1421\t\n1422\t 2 \" qa_id \": string , \n1423\t\n1424\t 3 \" qa_type \": \" event_presence | event_attribute | speech_content \", \n1425\t\n1426\t 4 \" answer_type \": \" two_choice \", \n1427\t\n1428\t 5 \" question \": string , \n1429\t\n1430\t 6 \" choices \": {\" A \": \" Yes \", \" B \": \" No \"}, \n1431\t\n1432\t 7 \" answer \": \" A \", \n1433\t\n1434\t 8 \" modality \": \" SFX | BGM | Speech | Scene \", \n1435\t\n1436\t 9 \" event_id \": int , \n1437\t\n1438\t 10 \" event_text \": string , \n1439\t\n1440\t 11 \" attribute_field \": string , \n1441\t\n1442\t 12 \" attribute_value \": string , \n1443\t\n1444\t 13 \" evidence \": object \n1445\t\n1446\t 14 } \n1447\t\n1448\t Target generation. \n1449\t\n1450\t For each annotated event, we first construct structured QA targets. For non-speech events, we create an event_presence target. For all events, we select informative attributes according to modality-specific priority lists and create event_attribute targets. Generic or uninformative values are removed. For speech events, if the action_or_content field contains explicit spoken content, we create a speech_content target. \n1451\t\n1452\t The attribute priorities are: \n1453\t\n1454\t • \n1455\t\n1456\t Speech : speaker gender, age, role, language, emotion, style, prosody, source. \n1457\t\n1458\t • \n1459\t\n1460\t SFX : source, action/content, intensity, spatial property, temporal pattern, material, style. \n1461\t\n1462\t • \n1463\t\n1464\t BGM : genre, mood, instrument, style, intensity, temporal pattern, spatial property. \n1465\t\n1466\t • \n1467\t\n1468\t Scene : spatial property, temporal pattern, intensity, mood, style. \n1469\t\n1470\t Question rewriting. \n1471\t\n1472\t Structured targets are rewritten into natural questions using an LLM with strict constraints. The model is instructed to preserve the qa_id , qa_type , event ID, modality, and attribute field; not to add or remove targets; and not to include answer choices in the question. The prompt also prevents exposing internal annotation terms such as actor_or_source or action_or_content . For speech_content , the rewritten question must quote the exact spoken content or a long exact phrase. \n1473\t\n1474\t The core rewriting instruction is: \n1475\t\n1476\t ⬇ \n1477\t\n1478\t 1 Rewrite each structured QA target as one natural yes / no question for evaluating a generated audio clip . \n1479\t\n1480\t 2 \n1481\t\n1482\t 3 Requirements : \n1483\t\n1484\t 4 - Answerable solely from the audio . \n1485\t\n1486\t 5 - Preserve the original target exactly . \n1487\t\n1488\t 6 - Do not add or omit information . \n1489\t\n1490\t 7 - Do not expose internal field names . \n1491\t\n1492\t 8 - Keep the question concise and natural . \n1493\t\n1494\t 9 \n1495\t\n1496\t 10 Return strict JSON : \n1497\t\n1498\t 11 { \n1499\t\n1500\t 12 \" questions \": [ \n1501\t\n1502\t 13 { \n1503\t\n1504\t 14 \" qa_id \": \" same id as input \", \n1505\t\n1506\t 15 \" question \": \" natural yes / no question \" \n1507\t\n1508\t 16 } \n1509\t\n1510\t 17 ] \n1511\t\n1512\t 18 } \n1513\t\n1514\t Leakage prevention and fallback templates. \n1515\t\n1516\t To reduce question leakage, we apply rule-based checks after rewriting. A question is replaced by a safe template if it copies the full event text into an attribute question, repeats the attribute value excessively, exposes internal field names, or contains forbidden words such as reference , annotation , caption , or metadata . We use component-aware fallback templates, e.g., \n1517\t\n1518\t ⬇ \n1519\t\n1520\t 1 Event presence : Is there a sound event described as { event_text }? \n1521\t\n1522\t 2 BGM genre : Is there { value } background music in the audio ? \n1523\t\n1524\t 3 SFX material : Does the sound have a { value } quality ? \n1525\t\n1526\t 4 Speech language : Is the speech in { value }? \n1527\t\n1528\t 5 Speech content : Does the speaker say \"{ spoken_content }\" in the audio ? \n1529\t\n1530\t Validation and filtering. \n1531\t\n1532\t Finally, each generated question is validated against its structured evidence. The validation checks whether the question refers to the same event, modality, and attribute, whether the attribute value is correctly represented, and whether unsupported details are introduced. Questions with severe validation errors are discarded. We also remove duplicated questions, questions with internal field wording, invalid Scene event-attribute questions, and malformed questions. After filtering, the final benchmark contains 25,707 rubrics pairs over 2,258 audio-text instances. \n1533\t\n1534\t Detailed Metric Definitions \n1535\t\n1536\t This section provides the precise definitions of the fine-grained metrics used in AudioScape-TTA. Unless otherwise stated, all reported values are multiplied by 100 100 and presented as percentages. \n1537\t\n1538\t Semantic Satisfaction Rate. \n1539\t\n1540\t For each event-presence or event-attribute rubric i i , let\n1541\t p i ∈ { Yes , No } p_{i}\\in\\{\\mathrm{Yes},\\mathrm{No}\\} denote the semantic answer predicted by\n1542\tQwen3-Omni-Instruct and let y i = Yes y_{i}=\\mathrm{Yes} denote the positive target\n1543\trequirement derived from the annotation. Its binary satisfaction indicator is \n1544\t\n1545\t s i = 𝕀 ​ [ p i = y i ] . s_{i}=\\mathbb{I}[p_{i}=y_{i}]. \n1546\t\n1547\t For a speech-content rubric i i , the satisfaction indicator is defined using\n1548\tthe ASR-based coverage criterion: \n1549\t\n1550\t s i = 𝕀 ​ [ R mixed , i ≥ 0.60 ] . s_{i}=\\mathbb{I}[R_{\\mathrm{mixed},i}\\geq 0.60]. \n1551\t\n1552\t For any rubric set 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} , the Semantic Satisfaction Rate is the\n1553\tmicro-average of item-level indicators:\n1554\t\n1555\t SR ​ ( 𝒮 ) \\displaystyle\\mathrm{SR}(\\mathcal{S}) \n1556\t = 1 | 𝒮 | ​ ∑ i ∈ 𝒮 s i , \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}}s_{i}, \n1557\t\n1558\t (5) \n1559\t\n1560\t Overall ​ SR \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Overall\\ SR} \n1561\t = SR ​ ( 𝒬 ) , Event ​ SR = SR ​ ( 𝒬 event ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{SR}(\\mathcal{Q}),\\qquad\\mathrm{Event\\ SR}=\\mathrm{SR}(\\mathcal{Q}_{\\mathrm{event}}), \n1562\t\n1563\t Attribute ​ SR \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Attribute\\ SR} \n1564\t = SR ​ ( 𝒬 attr ) . \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{SR}(\\mathcal{Q}_{\\mathrm{attr}}). \n1565\t\n1566\t (6) \n1567\t\n1568\t Evaluator confidence. \n1569\t\n1570\t For event-presence and event-attribute rubrics, we additionally report an uncalibrated evaluator-confidence diagnostic. Let ℓ A \\ell_{A} and ℓ B \\ell_{B} be the log-probabilities assigned to the two answer options. We normalize them over the binary choice set: \n1571\t\n1572\t q A = exp ⁡ ( ℓ A ) exp ⁡ ( ℓ A ) + exp ⁡ ( ℓ B ) , q B = exp ⁡ ( ℓ B ) exp ⁡ ( ℓ A ) + exp ⁡ ( ℓ B ) . q_{A}=\\frac{\\exp(\\ell_{A})}{\\exp(\\ell_{A})+\\exp(\\ell_{B})},\\quad q_{B}=\\frac{\\exp(\\ell_{B})}{\\exp(\\ell_{A})+\\exp(\\ell_{B})}. \n1573\t\n1574\t (7) \n1575\t\n1576\t and the reported value is the mean confidence over the corresponding rubrics subset. This quantity measures the evaluator’s relative preference between the two candidate answers only. It is not calibrated as a probability of semantic correctness, is not an audio-quality metric, and should not be compared directly across different evaluator models. \n1577\t\n1578\t Speech-content preprocessing. \n1579\t\n1580\t Speech content is evaluated by comparing the Qwen3-ASR transcript with the annotated target text. Before scoring, we remove a leading bracketed tag when present, lowercase Latin text, and collapse consecutive whitespace. Punctuation does not form an evaluation unit. We retain numbers, repeated words, and filler expressions; no stop-word removal, duplicate suppression, or prefix-based shortcut is applied. \n1581\t\n1582\t We extract two unit types from the normalized text: \n1583\t\n1584\t • \n1585\t\n1586\t Word units : lowercased Latin alphanumeric tokens, optionally containing internal hyphens or apostrophes; \n1587\t\n1588\t • \n1589\t\n1590\t Character units : individual Chinese CJK characters. \n1591\t\n1592\t This mixed-unit formulation supports English, Chinese, and code-switched speech under a single protocol. \n1593\t\n1594\t Mixed-unit coverage. \n1595\t\n1596\t Let r r and r ^ \\hat{r} denote the normalized reference text and ASR transcript, respectively. Let c r w ​ ( u ) c_{r}^{w}(u) and c r ^ w ​ ( u ) c_{\\hat{r}}^{w}(u) be the occurrence counts of word unit u u in the reference and transcript; c r c ​ ( v ) c_{r}^{c}(v) and c r ^ c ​ ( v ) c_{\\hat{r}}^{c}(v) are defined analogously for Chinese character unit v v . We use clipped-count matching: \n1597\t\n1598\t m w = ∑ u min ⁡ ( c r w ​ ( u ) , c r ^ w ​ ( u ) ) , m_{w}=\\sum_{u}\\min\\left(c_{r}^{w}(u),c_{\\hat{r}}^{w}(u)\\right), \n1599\t\n1600\t (8) \n1601\t\n1602\t m c = ∑ v min ⁡ ( c r c ​ ( v ) , c r ^ c ​ ( v ) ) . m_{c}=\\sum_{v}\\min\\left(c_{r}^{c}(v),c_{\\hat{r}}^{c}(v)\\right). \n1603\t\n1604\t (9) \n1605\t\n1606\t The total number of matched units is m = m w + m c m=m_{w}+m_{c} . Let N r N_{r} denote the total number of word and character units in the reference text. We define mixed-unit coverage as \n1607\t\n1608\t R mixed = m N r . R_{\\mathrm{mixed}}=\\frac{m}{N_{r}}. \n1609\t\n1610\t (10) \n1611\t\n1612\t Clipped-count matching prevents a repeated transcript unit from matching\n1613\tmultiple occurrences in the reference. Importantly, this score is an\n1614\torder-agnostic reference-unit coverage measure: it does not require the\n1615\tmatched units to occur contiguously or in the same order as in the\n1616\treference, and it does not penalize additional transcript units. This\n1617\tcoverage score is used only to determine SCCA@0.60; mixed-unit precision\n1618\tand F1 are not reported as benchmark metrics. \n1619\t\n1620\t Speech Content satisfaction rate. \n1621\t\n1622\t Our primary speech metric is Speech Content satisfaction rate at a mixed-unit coverage threshold τ \\tau , denoted as SCCA@ τ \\tau . A speech-content item is counted as correct if its mixed-unit coverage reaches the threshold: \n1623\t\n1624\t s i speech = 𝟏 ​ ( R mixed , i ≥ τ ) . s_{i}^{\\mathrm{speech}}=\\mathbf{1}\\left(R_{\\mathrm{mixed},i}\\geq\\tau\\right). \n1625\t\n1626\t (11) \n1627\t\n1628\t For the N speech N_{\\mathrm{speech}} speech-content items, we compute \n1629\t\n1630\t SCCA ​ @ ​ τ = 1 N speech ​ ∑ i = 1 N speech 𝟏 ​ ( R mixed , i ≥ τ ) . \\mathrm{SCCA@}\\tau=\\frac{1}{N_{\\mathrm{speech}}}\\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\\mathrm{speech}}}\\mathbf{1}\\left(R_{\\mathrm{mixed},i}\\geq\\tau\\right). \n1631\t\n1632\t (12) \n1633\t\n1634\t We use τ = 0.60 \\tau=0.60 throughout the paper. Consequently, SCCA@0.60 measures whether the transcript contains clipped\n1635\tmatches for at least 60 % 60\\% of the reference units under the mixed-unit\n1636\tcoverage criterion. It is not an order-sensitive exact-transcription\n1637\tmetric. The score pools clipped matches over Latin word units and Chinese\n1638\tcharacter units; it does not use a maximum across unit types or any\n1639\tfixed-length prefix shortcut. \n1640\t\n1641\t Supplementary WER and CER diagnostics. \n1642\t\n1643\t WER and CER are computed from the same Qwen3-ASR transcripts used for\n1644\tspeech-content evaluation. In contrast to SCCA@0.60, which is a rubric-level\n1645\tbinary target-coverage metric over N speech = 163 N_{\\mathrm{speech}}=163 speech-content\n1646\trubrics, WER and CER are clip-level corpus transcription diagnostics. \n1647\t\n1648\t Consistent with the main-text statistics, the final benchmark contains 163\n1649\tspeech-content rubrics associated with 83 clips containing target utterances.\n1650\tFor the supplementary clip-level transcription diagnostics, we construct a\n1651\tcaption-derived reference transcript by concatenating quoted\n1652\t [Speaker ...] utterances in annotation order. This construction is\n1653\tavailable for 80 of the 83 target-utterance clips. The remaining three clips\n1654\tcontain speech-related targets without a usable quoted utterance reference\n1655\t(e.g., indistinct or non-lexical vocal activity) and are excluded only from\n1656\tthe WER/CER computation; they remain included in the speech-content benchmark\n1657\tand in SCCA@0.60. \n1658\t\n1659\t In the final evaluation cache, WER is computed over\n1660\t | 𝒥 WER | = 69 |\\mathcal{J}_{\\mathrm{WER}}|=69 valid reference clips containing a total of\n1661\t 1 , 503 1{,}503 Latin word units, and CER is computed over\n1662\t | 𝒥 CER | = 11 |\\mathcal{J}_{\\mathrm{CER}}|=11 valid reference clips containing a total of\n1663\t 309 309 Chinese CJK character units. A code-switched clip may contribute to both\n1664\tsets when it contains both unit types. Because CER is computed on a smaller\n1665\tChinese-reference subset, it should be interpreted as a complementary\n1666\tdiagnostic with additional caution. The resulting WER and CER values are\n1667\treported in Table  10 . \n1668\t\n1669\t Modality, composition, and complexity subsets. \n1670\t\n1671\t For modality-level analysis, rubrics items are grouped according to the queried modality, including scene, sound effects, background music, and speech. In particular, Speech-modality rubrics satisfaction rate denotes rubrics satisfaction rate over rubrics associated with the speech modality; it is distinct from SCCA@0.60, which evaluates the linguistic content of synthesized speech through ASR transcripts. \n1672\t\n1673\t For composition analysis, samples are grouped by the set of modalities required by their prompts, such as Sound-only, Sound+Music, or Sound+Speech+Music. For complexity analysis, samples are grouped into Easy, Medium, and Hard subsets according to the benchmark’s event-density and structural-complexity annotations. All subset results use the same item-level correctness definition as the overall rubrics-based evaluation. \n1674\t\n1675\t Complementary global metrics. \n1676\t\n1677\t We additionally report global quality and alignment metrics as complementary measurements. Audiobox-Aesthetic provides content enjoyment (CE), content usefulness (CU), production complexity (PC), and production quality (PQ), where higher values indicate better predicted perceptual quality. 𝐂𝐋𝐀𝐏 MS \\mathbf{CLAP}_{\\mathrm{MS}} measures global text–audio embedding similarity, where higher values indicate stronger coarse semantic alignment. Distribution-based metrics include Fréchet distances computed in pretrained audio-feature spaces, Kullback–Leibler divergence (KL), and Inception Score (ISC). Lower Fréchet distance and KL are preferred, whereas higher ISC is preferred. These global metrics are not included in SR and are intended to complement, rather than replace, the proposed fine-grained semantic evaluation. \n1678\t\n1679\t Model \n1680\t Paradigm \n1681\t Text Encoder \n1682\t Audio Representation \n1683\t Generation Backbone \n1684\t\n1685\t AudioLDM 2 \n1686\t LD \n1687\t CLAP + FLAN-T5 \n1688\t AudioMAE + Mel VAE \n1689\t UNet \n1690\t\n1691\t Make-An-Audio 2 \n1692\t LD \n1693\t CLAP + T5 \n1694\t Mel VAE \n1695\t Transformer \n1696\t\n1697\t AudioGen \n1698\t Autoregressive \n1699\t T5 Conditioner \n1700\t EnCodec Tokenizer \n1701\t AR Transformer \n1702\t\n1703\t Tango 2 \n1704\t LD + DPO \n1705\t FLAN-T5 \n1706\t AudioLDM VAE \n1707\t UNet \n1708\t\n1709\t TangoFlux \n1710\t Flow Matching \n1711\t FLAN-T5 \n1712\t Stable Audio VAE \n1713\t Flux DiT \n1714\t\n1715\t EzAudio \n1716\t DiT \n1717\t FLAN-T5 \n1718\t Stable Audio VAE + DAC \n1719\t EzAudio-DiT \n1720\t\n1721\t MAGNeT \n1722\t Masked Modeling \n1723\t T5-Large \n1724\t EnCodec Tokenizer \n1725\t Non-AR Transformer \n1726\t\n1727\t Stable Audio Open \n1728\t DiT \n1729\t T5-Base \n1730\t Waveform VAE \n1731\t Diffusion Transformer \n1732\t\n1733\t MMAudio \n1734\t Flow Matching \n1735\t CLIP \n1736\t Mel VAE + BigVGAN \n1737\t MM-DiT \n1738\t\n1739\t Foley-Omni \n1740\t MM Flow Matching \n1741\t UM-T5 \n1742\t Mel VAE + BigVGAN \n1743\t Conditional DiT \n1744\t\n1745\t Omni2Sound \n1746\t Unified Diffusion \n1747\t FLAN-T5 \n1748\t Wav VAE \n1749\t DiT \n1750\t\n1751\t AudioStory \n1752\t LLM-guided Diffusion \n1753\t Qwen2.5 + T5 \n1754\t Whisper + VAE \n1755\t LLM Planner + DiT \n1756\t\n1757\t Dasheng AudioGen \n1758\t Unified Non-AR \n1759\t FLAN-T5-Large \n1760\t Dasheng Tokenizer \n1761\t Flow DiT \n1762\t\n1763\t Table 7: \n1764\tArchitectural overview of the 13 open-source text-to-audio (TTA) models evaluated in AudioScape-TTA.\n1765\tWe summarize the generation paradigms, text encoders, audio representations, and generation backbones.\n1766\tLD denotes Latent Diffusion, DPO denotes Diffusion Preference Optimization,\n1767\tDiT denotes Diffusion Transformer, AR denotes Autoregressive, and Non-AR denotes Non-Autoregressive generation.\n1768\tFor multimodal systems, only components relevant to text-to-audio generation are listed.\n1769\t\n1770\t Human Evaluation Protocol \n1771\t\n1772\t To validate the effectiveness of our automatic evaluation framework, we\n1773\tconduct a human evaluation study on a subset of AudioScape-TTA. We randomly\n1774\tselect 10 prompts covering different modality compositions and complexity\n1775\tlevels, and collect generated audio from all 13 evaluated TTA models,\n1776\tresulting in 130 audio samples. The identities of the models are anonymized,\n1777\tand all samples are randomly shuffled before evaluation. \n1778\t\n1779\t Each audio sample is independently rated by 26 participants. For each sample,\n1780\tparticipants evaluate three aspects: event completeness ,\n1781\t attribute satisfaction , and audio quality . Event completeness\n1782\tmeasures whether the required sound events described in the prompt are present\n1783\tin the generated audio. Attribute satisfaction rate evaluates whether fine-grained\n1784\tacoustic properties, such as sound source characteristics, temporal patterns,\n1785\tand other specified attributes, are correctly realized. Audio quality measures\n1786\toverall perceptual quality, including naturalness, coherence, and generation\n1787\tartifacts. \n1788\t\n1789\t Participants are provided with the original text prompt and the generated\n1790\taudio, and rate each dimension independently using a 5-point Likert scale.\n1791\tFor semantic dimensions ( event completeness and\n1792\t attribute satisfaction rate ), a higher score indicates that the generated\n1793\taudio better satisfies the corresponding textual requirements. For\n1794\t audio quality , a higher score indicates better perceptual quality\n1795\tand fewer noticeable artifacts. The final human score for each sample is\n1796\tcomputed by averaging the ratings from all participants. These human\n1797\tjudgments are then used to analyze the correlation between human perception\n1798\tand automatic evaluation metrics. \n1799\t\n1800\t Model \n1801\t 𝐅𝐃 VGG ↓ \\mathbf{FD}_{\\mathrm{VGG}}\\downarrow \n1802\t 𝐅𝐃 PANN ↓ \\mathbf{FD}_{\\mathrm{PANN}}\\downarrow \n1803\t 𝐅𝐃 PASST ↓ \\mathbf{FD}_{\\mathrm{PASST}}\\downarrow \n1804\t 𝐊𝐋 PANN ↓ \\mathbf{KL}_{\\mathrm{PANN}}\\downarrow \n1805\t 𝐊𝐋 PaSST ↓ \\mathbf{KL}_{\\mathrm{PaSST}}\\downarrow \n1806\t 𝐈𝐒𝐂 PANN ↑ \\mathbf{ISC}_{\\mathrm{PANN}}\\uparrow \n1807\t 𝐈𝐒𝐂 PASST ↑ \\mathbf{ISC}_{\\mathrm{PASST}}\\uparrow \n1808\t\n1809\t AudioLDM 2 \n1810\t 4.816 \n1811\t 15.342 \n1812\t 229.479 \n1813\t 1.787 \n1814\t 1.694 \n1815\t 8.157 \n1816\t 7.600 \n1817\t\n1818\t Make-An-Audio 2 \n1819\t 5.580 \n1820\t 21.137 \n1821\t 245.892 \n1822\t 2.477 \n1823\t 2.432 \n1824\t 5.481 \n1825\t 5.900 \n1826\t\n1827\t AudioGen \n1828\t 3.976 \n1829\t 2.874 \n1830\t 155.277 \n1831\t 2.064 \n1832\t 1.930 \n1833\t 7.255 \n1834\t 7.648 \n1835\t\n1836\t Tango 2 \n1837\t 3.965 \n1838\t 13.619 \n1839\t 227.666 \n1840\t 1.919 \n1841\t 1.625 \n1842\t 7.194 \n1843\t 6.306 \n1844\t\n1845\t TangoFlux \n1846\t 4.504 \n1847\t 19.405 \n1848\t 266.794 \n1849\t 1.990 \n1850\t 1.755 \n1851\t 8.344 \n1852\t 7.283 \n1853\t\n1854\t EzAudio \n1855\t 2.514 \n1856\t 9.711 \n1857\t 119.163 \n1858\t 1.851 \n1859\t 1.740 \n1860\t 8.291 \n1861\t 8.515 \n1862\t\n1863\t MAGNeT \n1864\t 15.711 \n1865\t 38.295 \n1866\t 509.685 \n1867\t 4.015 \n1868\t 3.324 \n1869\t 1.913 \n1870\t 1.736 \n1871\t\n1872\t Stable Audio Open \n1873\t 3.114 \n1874\t 14.089 \n1875\t 199.270 \n1876\t 2.035 \n1877\t 1.91