, Marco Tagliasacchi, Emily Xue, Spandana Raj Babbula, Bo Pang, Nan Ding, Gloria Shen, Elijah Peake, Remi Crocker, Shubha Srinivas Raghvendra, Danny Swisher, Woohyun Han, Richa Singh, Ling Wu, Vladimir Pchelin, Tsendsuren Munkhdalai, Dana Alon, Geoff Bacon, Efren Robles, Jannis Bulian, Melvin Johnson, George Powell, Felipe Tiengo Ferreira, Yaoyiran Li, Frederik Benzing, Mihajlo Velimirović, Hubert Soyer, William Kong, Tony, Nguyên, Zhen Yang, Jeremiah Liu, Joost van Amersfoort, Daniel Gillick, Baochen Sun, Nathalie Rauschmayr, Katie Zhang, Serena Zhan, Tao Zhou, Alexey Frolov, Chengrun Yang, Denis Vnukov, Louis Rouillard, Hongji Li, Amol Mandhane, Nova Fallen, Rajesh Venkataraman, Clar", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false, "persistedOutputPath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/17cabbeb-4110-4879-b2c6-8c2ab37431f0/tool-results/b38d96jsh.txt", "persistedOutputSize": 132908}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785997086317", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae97c369e62eb566", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "curl -sL \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03116\" -o /tmp/anyaudio_abs.html 2>&1; python3 -c \"\nimport re\nwith open('/tmp/anyaudio_abs.html') as f: h=f.read()\nh=re.sub(r'<script[^>]*>.*?</script>','',h,flags=re.DOTALL)\nh=re.sub(r'<style[^>]*>.*?</style>','',h,flags=re.DOTALL)\nh=re.sub(r'<[^>]+>',' ',h)\nh=re.sub(r'&nbsp;',' ',h); h=re.sub(r'&amp;','&',h); h=re.sub(r'&#x27;',\\\"'\\\",h); h=re.sub(r'&quot;','\\\"',h)\nh=re.sub(r'\\n\\s*\\n','\\n\\n',h); h=re.sub(r' +',' ',h)\n# Find abstract block\nm=re.search(r'Abstract:(.*?)Subjects:',h,re.DOTALL)\nif m: print('ABSTRACT:',m.group(1).strip()[:3000])\nm2=re.search(r'Submitted on (.*?)[\\)\\]]',h)\nif m2: print('DATE:',m2.group(1))\n# find authors\nm3=re.search(r'Authors?:(.*?)Abstract',h,re.DOTALL)\nif m3: print('AUTHORS:',m3.group(1).strip()[:500])\n\" 2>&1 | head -40", "description": "Extract AnyAudio-Judge abstract and authors"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "17cabbeb-4110-4879-b2c6-8c2ab37431f0", "uuid": "80a3556e-53b4-4c2a-a192-0af36a1ce1f5"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae97c369e62eb566", "type": "tool_result", "content": "ABSTRACT: The rapid advancement of instruction-guided audio generation has highlighted the critical need for robust alignment evaluation. Current automated evaluation methods heavily rely on holistic scoring from general-purpose large language models, which struggle to decouple complex instructions, lack interpretability, and fail to capture fine-grained attribute mismatches. To address this, we introduce a novel dynamic rubric-based evaluation paradigm that adaptively decomposes complex audio captions into a variable number of independent, verifiable binary rubric items. To rigorously benchmark this capability, we propose the AnyAudio-Judge Bench, a comprehensive, bilingual benchmark comprising 7,920 meticulously curated samples across four diverse audio domains (speech, sound, music, and mixed), featuring deliberately constructed hard negatives. Furthermore, we construct a large-scale corpus of 105K samples with explicit Chain-of-Thought (CoT) rationales to train our dedicated evaluator, the AnyAudio-Judge model. By employing a training pipeline that combines Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), our model successfully aligns its reasoning paths with the rubric-based scoring mechanism. Extensive experiments demonstrate that AnyAudio-Judge not only significantly enhances zero-shot alignment detection compared to state-of-the-art baselines, but also provides precise and interpretable reward signals that substantially improve instruction alignment in downstream reinforcement learning for audio generation.\nDATE: 2 Jun 2026\nAUTHORS: Haitao Li , Tian Tan , Yuguang Yang , Shan Yang , Xie Chen View a PDF of the paper titled AnyAudio-Judge: A Dynamic Rubric-Based Benchmark and Evaluator for Audio Instruction Following, by Haitao Li and 4 other authors \n View PDF \n HTML (experimental)", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "17cabbeb-4110-4879-b2c6-8c2ab37431f0", "uuid": "c354bd60-24b2-4b98-9eb5-9c66e352b3a7", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T06:18:29.548Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "ABSTRACT: The rapid advancement of instruction-guided audio generation has highlighted the critical need for robust alignment evaluation. Current automated evaluation methods heavily rely on holistic scoring from general-purpose large language models, which struggle to decouple complex instructions, lack interpretability, and fail to capture fine-grained attribute mismatches. To address this, we introduce a novel dynamic rubric-based evaluation paradigm that adaptively decomposes complex audio captions into a variable number of independent, verifiable binary rubric items. To rigorously benchmark this capability, we propose the AnyAudio-Judge Bench, a comprehensive, bilingual benchmark comprising 7,920 meticulously curated samples across four diverse audio domains (speech, sound, music, and mixed), featuring deliberately constructed hard negatives. Furthermore, we construct a large-scale corpus of 105K samples with explicit Chain-of-Thought (CoT) rationales to train our dedicated evaluator, the AnyAudio-Judge model. By employing a training pipeline that combines Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), our model successfully aligns its reasoning paths with the rubric-based scoring mechanism. Extensive experiments demonstrate that AnyAudio-Judge not only significantly enhances zero-shot alignment detection compared to state-of-the-art baselines, but also provides precise and interpretable reward signals that substantially improve instruction alignment in downstream reinforcement learning for audio generation.\nDATE: 2 Jun 2026\nAUTHORS: Haitao Li , Tian Tan , Yuguang Yang , Shan Yang , Xie Chen View a PDF of the paper titled AnyAudio-Judge: A Dynamic Rubric-Based Benchmark and Evaluator for Audio Instruction Following, by Haitao Li and 4 other authors \n View PDF \n HTML (experimental)", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785997130671", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "关键发现。AnyAudio-Judge (2026年6月2日) 与本文（2026年8月5日）属于同期工作——间隔2个月，因此根据技能规则，不作为新颖性扣分依据。然而，作者存在重叠（Yuguang Yang, Shan Yang, Xie Chen 出现在两篇论文中），且两者都使用了基于评分标准（rubric-based）的音频评估。这是一个重要的背景信息。\n\n现在，让我从论文文本中获取评估器偏差/合理性检查章节（我还没看过），并完成剩余的研究。"}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "17cabbeb-4110-4879-b2c6-8c2ab37431f0", "uuid": "464384f1-f2c6-4a12-a41d-1eeaa3a126ed"}
